 0.01430511474609375, 0.016632080078125, 0.032257080078125, -0.026031494140625, -0.02081298828125, 0.005725860595703125, -0.01432037353515625, -0.002826690673828125, -0.0021724700927734375, 0.01387786865234375, 0.006683349609375, 0.00666046142578125, -0.01727294921875, 0.0028934478759765625, 0.034912109375, 0.0309295654296875, 0.0026569366455078125, -0.0247802734375, 0.00531005859375, -0.004360198974609375, 0.0175323486328125, -0.01386260986328125, 0.0011463165283203125, -0.0013017654418945312, -0.013458251953125, 0.01062774658203125, 0.00428009033203125, 0.00921630859375, -0.0004603862762451172, 0.0278472900390625, 0.031646728515625, 4.941225051879883e-05, -0.01442718505859375, -0.002422332763671875, 0.01377105712890625, 0.004611968994140625, 0.01163482666015625, 0.00555419921875, -0.0232391357421875, 0.002170562744140625, -0.01479339599609375, -0.01085662841796875, -0.00785064697265625, -0.0297698974609375, 0.01026153564453125, -0.0625, 0.0004367828369140625, -0.0189971923828125, -0.022552490234375, 0.0087890625, -0.03167724609375, -0.007747650146484375, -0.012451171875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature [1,2,3]. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked, despite their importance for downstream applications such as continual learning, autonomous decision-making, and safety-critical systems. Reliable agents are essential to ensure consistent performance, prevent regressions, and enable trustworthy deployment in real-world scenarios.\n\n**Definitions**\n\nFor clarity, we define key terms as follows:\n- **Reliability:** The degree to which an agent consistently achieves high performance across repeated trials and varying conditions, operationalized as low variance in task completion rates and absence of regressions.\n- **Robustness:** The agent\u2019s ability to maintain stable performance (i.e., low standard deviation in completion rates, no catastrophic failures) under perturbations such as stochasticity, task order changes, or noisy feedback. We consider an agent robust if its performance variance across seeds and permutations is less than 10% and it avoids abrupt drops (>20%) in task completion.\n- **Fragility:** The susceptibility of an agent to performance degradation or failure modes (e.g., catastrophic forgetting, spurious memory entries, abrupt performance drops) when exposed to minor changes in environment or task sequence. We operationalize fragility as observed performance drops >10% or emergence of new failure modes under perturbations.\n\n**Research Questions**\n\nWe address the following research questions:\n\n1. **Robustness:** To what extent do memory-based self-improving agents exhibit robustness\u2014defined above\u2014when exposed to stochasticity and changes in task order?\n2. **Fragility Factors:** What specific factors contribute to their observed fragility (i.e., susceptibility to performance degradation or failure under perturbations such as task order shuffling or noisy feedback)?\n3. **Evaluation Protocols:** How can evaluation protocols be improved to better capture these reliability issues?\n\nFor example, a fragile agent might suddenly fail previously mastered tasks after learning a new one (catastrophic forgetting), or record misleading information in its memory (spurious entries) that harms future performance.\n\n**Background**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods:\n- **Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA) [4]:** Store and retrieve textual summaries of past episodes to inform future decisions, emphasizing episodic recall.\n- **Self-Reflective Language Agents (SRLA) [5]:** Maintain a log of self-generated reflections and lessons learned after each task, focusing on meta-cognitive self-assessment.\n\nThese methods exemplify two contrasting approaches to memory construction\u2014episodic versus reflective\u2014and are widely used as baselines in recent benchmarks [6,7]. While these two agents do not exhaust the space of memory-based architectures, they capture key differences in memory structure and content that are hypothesized to affect robustness and reliability. We acknowledge that findings may not generalize to all agent types and discuss this limitation further below. Prior evaluations have primarily used fixed task sequences, often without reporting variance or sensitivity to task order, leaving open questions about generalizability and reproducibility.\n\n**Methods**\n\n*Experimental Setup*\n\n- **Agents:** EMAA and SRLA, as described above. Implementations follow the original papers, with open-source codebases adapted for our environment.\n- **Task Suite:** 10 multi-step reasoning and manipulation tasks, selected for diversity in required skills, environment feedback, and complexity. Tasks include:\n  - **Symbolic Reasoning:** Text-based logic puzzles [8], arithmetic word problems [9], sequence prediction [10], symbolic sorting (custom), rule induction (custom).\n  - **Embodied Manipulation:** Object arrangement [11], navigation [12], sorting in simulated 3D environments [13], object retrieval (custom), spatial reasoning (custom).\n  - *Custom Tasks:* For example, the symbolic sorting task requires the agent to sort a list of symbols by a hidden rule revealed through feedback; spatial reasoning involves arranging objects to satisfy spatial constraints. Full task descriptions and environment parameters are provided in *Supplementary Table 1*.\n- **Environment Setup:** All tasks are implemented in a unified simulation environment with standardized APIs for agent interaction and feedback. Each task provides explicit success/failure signals and logs all agent actions for reproducibility.\n\n- **Performance Metrics:** The primary metric is **task completion rate** (percentage of tasks successfully completed per run). For multi-step tasks, a task is scored as successful if all required steps are completed correctly; partial credit is not awarded. Secondary metrics include **mean accuracy** (for tasks with discrete outputs) and **F1 score** (for tasks with multiple correct outputs), as appropriate. All metrics are averaged over five independent random seeds, and 95% confidence intervals are reported for all key results.\n\n- **Experimental Procedure:**\n  - Each agent is evaluated on five independent random seeds to quantify performance variance and identify failure modes. Due to computational constraints, we sample a single random permutation of task order per run, rather than exhaustively enumerating all permutations. This approach provides a tractable compromise but limits statistical power; a power analysis (see *Supplementary Material*) indicates that with five seeds, we can detect effect sizes of d \u2265 1.0 with 80% power at \u03b1 = 0.05.\n  - Agent hyperparameters are set to default values as reported in the original papers: memory size = 100 entries, retrieval strategy = cosine similarity over sentence embeddings, learning rate = 1e-4, batch size = 16, and no additional tuning is performed.\n\n*Memory Analysis Rubric*\n\n- We manually inspect the agents\u2019 memory banks after each run, using a rubric that codes for:\n  - **Specificity:** Level of detail (e.g., explicit mention of actions and outcomes).\n  - **Relevance:** Applicability to future tasks (e.g., generalizable strategies).\n  - **Completeness:** Coverage of key events (e.g., whether all critical steps are recorded).\n- **Rubric Development:** The rubric was developed by reviewing prior work and iteratively piloting on a subset of memory entries to ensure clarity and coverage. Two independent annotators coded a random sample of 100 entries, achieving Cohen\u2019s kappa (average \u03ba = 0.81). Disagreements were resolved through discussion.\n- **Example Coded Entry:**  \n  - *Memory Entry:* \u201cTask: Sort objects by color. Action: Grouped all red items together. Outcome: Success. Lesson: Sorting by color works when colors are unique.\u201d  \n  - *Coding:* Specificity = high; Relevance = high; Completeness = full.\n- *Supplementary Table 2* provides the full rubric and additional examples.\n\n**Results**\n\n*Key Findings Linked to Research Questions*\n\n1. **Robustness (RQ1): Evaluation Noise and Failure Modes**\n   - Agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks. Across five runs:\n     - **EMAA:** Mean final task completion rates ranged from 62% to 74% (95% CI \u00b16%), with standard deviation 8\u201313%.\n     - **SRLA:** Mean final task completion rates ranged from 68% to 78% (95% CI \u00b16%), with standard deviation 10\u201315%.\n   - Effect sizes for performance variance between agents were moderate (Cohen\u2019s d = 0.7).\n   - Example failure modes:\n     - In one run, EMAA failed three previously solved tasks after encountering a novel navigation task (catastrophic forgetting).\n     - In another, SRLA recorded a memory entry stating \u201cSorting objects by color is always optimal,\u201d which led to repeated failures on tasks requiring sorting by size (spurious memory entry).\n   - Stacking a self-improving loop can further amplify this noise, occasionally leading to such failure modes.\n\n2. **Fragility Factors (RQ2): Task Order Dependence**\n   - The agent\u2019s improvement is highly dependent on task order. In some permutations, agents failed to improve on later tasks, with average performance drops of up to 20% (mean drop: 13% \u00b14% CI, p = 0.03, paired t-test) compared to the default curriculum order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\n3. **Evaluation Protocols (RQ3): Memory and Feedback Analysis**\n   - Manual examination of the agents\u2019 memory indicates that task and environment underspecification contribute to fragility:\n     - 37% (\u00b15% CI) of memory entries in the baseline condition omitted explicit task goals.\n     - 22% (\u00b14% CI) failed to record outcomes.\n   - Ambiguous environment feedback leads to misleading or incomplete memories, reducing generalizability across task variations.\n\n**Intervention**\n\n*Methods*\n\n- To validate these findings, we introduced:\n  - A more detailed memory rubric (requiring explicit mention of task goals, actions, and outcomes in each entry).\n  - Structured environment feedback (e.g., explicit success/failure signals and clarifying messages).\n- This intervention was operationalized by modifying the memory-writing module to enforce rubric compliance using template-based prompts (e.g., \u201cTask: [goal]. Action: [steps taken]. Outcome: [result]. Lesson: [reflection].\u201d) and updating the environment API to emit standardized feedback messages (e.g., \u201cSuccess: Object sorted by color,\u201d \u201cFailure: Incorrect navigation path\u201d). *Supplementary Material* provides implementation details and code.\n\n*Results*\n\n- While these changes improved memory quality and reduced ambiguity, they required manual engineering and may not generalize to all agent architectures or task domains without further adaptation. There is a risk of overfitting agent behavior to the rubric or structured feedback, potentially reducing generalizability to less-structured or novel environments.\n- **Impact:** Comparing performance degradation in shuffled task orders before and after the intervention:\n  - Average degradation reduced from 20% to 10% (mean post-intervention: 8% \u00b13"}}